<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf III, Count of Holstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1160">1160</a>–<a href="/years/1225">1225</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Adolf III of Schauenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf III (1160–1225) was count of Holstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a> from the house of Schauenburg. Around 1188 he granted merchants land to found a new town beside the old cathedral settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, the origin of the city's commercial growth, and obtained privileges for its trade. He took part in the Third Crusade and lost Holstein to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1201.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-iii-of-holstein.jpg" alt="Adolf III, Count of Holstein" width="320" height="670" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlotte Heineccius · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf_III._Graf_von_Schauenburg_-_Engelbert_Peiffer_-_Trostbr%C3%BCcke_Hamburg2_close.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolf was the only son of Count Adolf II of Holstein-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagria">Wagria</a> and succeeded him in 1164, at first under the guardianship of his mother, Mechthild of Schwarzburg-Käfernburg, daughter of Count Sizzo III of Schwarzburg-Käfernburg. He married Adelheid of Assel in 1182 and, after her death in 1185, Adelheid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a>. Of his five children, Adolf IV succeeded him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-of-schauenburg">Bruno of Schauenburg</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Adolf first supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, going with him against Philipp von Heinsberg, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and fighting at his side at the Battle of Halerfeld, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, on 1 August 1180, with Count Bernhard I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a>. From Henry he received rights in the Middle Weser that became the basis of the County of Schauenburg. Later in 1180 he left Henry, who drove him from Holstein, and joined Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, with whose help he regained his lands in 1181 after Henry's fall.</p><p>In 1188 Frederick refused Adolf's claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, but Adolf went with him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in August 1190 and returned home to defend Holstein against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, who had come back from exile. He went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> again in 1196 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of Henry VI and came home in 1198. His rule coincided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s attempts at expansion under Canute VI and his brother Valdemar II. After losing the Battle of Stellau in 1201 he was taken prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> by Valdemar, and in 1203 he renounced his title to Holstein to win his freedom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Released, Adolf retired to the County of Schauenburg and left the reconquest of Holstein to his son, Adolf IV. In about 1224, at the request of Konrad von Rüdenberg, Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, he gave up the Vogtei of Wennigsen Abbey; the deed is the first surviving record of that monastery. He died on 3 January 1225. He is chiefly remembered for the settlement of traders he founded on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alster">Alster</a> near the Neue Burg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolf III of Holstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its revival came under the Schauenburg counts of Holstein: in about 1188 Count <b>Adolf III</b> granted a group of merchants land to found a new town (Neustadt) beside the old episcopal settlement, with a harbour on the Alster.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Germany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 395 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adolf III, Count of Holstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51,10&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
